### Justification
The claim that "it's unclear how Joe Biden acquired his recent wealth since leaving office in 2017" is directly contradicted by extensive public documentation. Multiple reputable news organizations, including the [LA Times](https://www.sandiegouniontribune.com/news/us-politics/la-na-pol-2020-joe-biden-income-20190709-story.html), [Boston Globe/AP](https://www.bostonglobe.com/news/politics/2019/07/09/biden-earned-millions-after-leaving-white-house-financial-disclosures-show/YDpLcGshpG7pJNzrTTRgWO/story.html), and [VOA News](https://www.voanews.com/a/usa_us-politics_biden-and-wife-made-more-15m-after-leaving-office/6171514.html), have thoroughly reported on Biden's post-Vice Presidency income based on his publicly released 2016-2018 tax returns and OGE financial disclosure forms. These documents clearly identify his income sources: approximately $13.2 million from book deals, $4.29 million from 47 paid speaking engagements, over $775,000 from his University of Pennsylvania professorship at the Penn Biden Center, and a federal pension of around $248,670 per year. His total income jumped from less than $400,000 annually as Vice President to more than $11 million in 2017 alone. There is no evidence of undisclosed income, hidden assets, or opaque financial activity—his financial disclosures were public, transparent, and detailed. The claim falsely implies mystery or opacity where the sources of Biden's wealth are well-documented and clearly identified in public records.